enue and place of  people that didn't know we were playing  and stuff like that and you've got to  kind of like slightly engineer these  things but um yeah it's hard you've got  you've just got you've got to feel it  though I think you've got to feel it  you've got to if you're if you're  excited about your your music then it'll  come through  how did that go there did everyone just  go hang on that suede  well the cat got out of the bag um yeah  it was actually and we did it in the  Moss Club in in Hackney and um and and  the defense chair Manchester and they  were kind of mad little gigs because the  fans actually ended up finding it  finding out his ass and and turning up  um but the idea was to do it in front of  one man and his dog  I don't think that's ever going to  happen again to you let's face it and  you've talked about this being a loud  noisy nasty Rock record but you've said  it's got the lyrical vulnerability that  comes with being an older man yeah and  some people might be surprised that you  think of yourself as being more  vulnerable as you get older some people  say surely you just get more  self-assured more confident with  experience well I don't know about  everyone else I kind of know speed for  myself but I don't find I think there's  a there's a kind of there's a sort of  assumption that as people's lives get  more comfortable in one sense that they  get more comfortable as people and in  one sense you know I'm kind of 55 I had  a fam I have a family I love and I'm  relatively comfortable in that sense but  I think there's there's kind of creeping  anxiety that you only have as a  middle-aged person you know kind of like  worries about the fragility of family  worries about mortality these kind of  things  um and I sort of wanted to reflect that  so I wanted it to be a rock record but  not from the perspective of a 25 year  old man very much from the perspective  of a 55 year old man I thought that was  quite an interesting kind of  um sort